    "result_text": "There is only one test class, and the full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` field for owners. It returns `true` when the owner's city already holds 40\u201349 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50), while the existing hard 409 rejection at 50 is unchanged.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema (alongside `bulkSignupWarning`), so the generated `OwnerDto` gets a `setCapacityWarning`.\n\n2. **`CityCapacity.java`** (new) \u2014 a small helper centralizing the city rules: `CAPACITY = 50`, `WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40`, a null-safe `count(city, repo)` (matching `RejectCityAtCapacity`'s population, soft-deleted included), and `warning(...)` = `count in [40, 49]`.\n\n3. **`RejectCityAtCapacity.java`** \u2014 refactored to reuse `CityCapacity.count`/`CityCapacity.CAPACITY` instead of a duplicate inline count; behavior (reject at \u2265 50) unchanged.\n\n4. **`RespondWithOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `ReplayCreatedOwner`** \u2014 each now sets `dto.setCapacityWarning(CityCapacity.warning(owner, ownerRepository))`, so the warning is computed at response time (like `bulkSignupWarning`) on GET, create, and idempotent replay.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 tests, 0 failures).",
